\title{A derivative-based regex engine for Reussir}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-11-16}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{design}
\tag{stdlib}
\tag{regex}
\meta{status}{draft}
\meta{created}{2026-11-16}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} draft, for discussion. The alphabet, the sets and the classes of decisions 2 and 5, is in review as \pr{1039}; nothing else is implemented.}

\p{A regex engine written in Reussir whose automaton is built from Brzozowski derivatives when the program runs. Its design follows Paguroidea, a lexer generator that builds its automaton when the program is compiled, and replaces each compile-time device of Paguroidea by a run-time one.}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-scope]{
\title{What the first version covers}

\ul{
\li{A regex is built with a tree builder. Parsing a pattern string is left to a later version.}
\li{The input is bytes, and the alphabet is a parameter: a unit is one byte, or one character decoded from UTF-8 as the matcher goes.}
\li{A matcher holds a list of rules, each a regex with an action. A match names the rule that wins and every other rule that matches the same text. This is the lexer form of Paguroidea: longest match, the earliest rule on a tie, anchored at the current position.}
\li{Input may arrive in pieces, for both alphabets.}
\li{A regex may use intersection and complement, as in Paguroidea.}
\li{Transitions are held in one compressed table per matcher, built completely when the rules are turned into a matcher, with vector acceleration for the states that loop on themselves.}
}

\p{Left to later versions: a pattern parser, search for a match anywhere in the input, captures, lookaround, counted repetition beyond what the builder expands, and Unicode property tables.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-example]{
\title{A worked example}

\p{Three rules over bytes:}

\pre{\verb~|
ident  = [a-z]+        action: Token::Ident
number = [0-9]+        action: Token::Number
space  = ' '+          action: Token::Space
~}

\p{\strong{Classes.} The sets the rules mention are \code{\verb~|[a-z]~}, \code{\verb~|[0-9]~} and \code{\verb~|' '~}. They split the 256 bytes into four classes that no rule can tell apart: \code{\verb~|0 = [a-z]~}, \code{\verb~|1 = [0-9]~}, \code{\verb~|2 = ' '~}, \code{\verb~|3 = everything else~}. A table of 256 entries maps a byte to its class.}

\p{\strong{States.} A state is one regex per rule. The start state is \code{\verb~|([a-z]+, [0-9]+, ' '+)~}. The derivative of a regex \code{\verb~|r~} by a class is a regex for what may follow one byte of that class. Deriving the start state by class 0 gives \code{\verb~|([a-z]*, ∅, ∅)~}, where \code{\verb~|∅~} matches nothing; its first component accepts the empty string, so the state accepts with the rule \code{\verb~|ident~}. Deriving that state by class 0 again gives the same state.}

\table{
\tr{\th{State}\th{regexes}\th{accepts}\th{class 0}\th{class 1}\th{class 2}\th{class 3}}
\tr{\td{0}\td{\code{\verb~|([a-z]+, [0-9]+, ' '+)~}}\td{}\td{1}\td{2}\td{3}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{\code{\verb~|([a-z]*, ∅, ∅)~}}\td{ident}\td{1}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{\code{\verb~|(∅, [0-9]*, ∅)~}}\td{number}\td{dead}\td{2}\td{dead}\td{dead}}
\tr{\td{3}\td{\code{\verb~|(∅, ∅, ' '*)~}}\td{space}\td{dead}\td{dead}\td{3}\td{dead}}
}

\p{\strong{Matching \code{\verb~|abc 42~}.} State 0, byte \code{\verb~|a~} is class 0, go to state 1, which accepts \code{\verb~|ident~}: remember "ident, length 1". \code{\verb~|b~}, \code{\verb~|c~} stay in state 1: "ident, length 3". The space leads to the dead state, so the answer is the last one remembered: the action of \code{\verb~|ident~}, 3 bytes.}

\p{\strong{Acceleration.} State 1 loops on itself for class 0 and leaves on every other byte. Its loop is one range, so the matcher can test 16 or more bytes at once with \code{\verb~|(b >= 'a') & (b <= 'z')~} on a vector and take \code{\verb~|first_set~} of the complement to find where the run ends. This is what Paguroidea emits as code for such a state; here the two bounds are data stored with the state.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-decisions]{
\title{Design decisions}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-normalization]{
\title{1. Regexes are normalized when they are constructed}

\p{Paguroidea derives a plain tree and normalizes it in a separate pass, and compares states as whole trees. The engines that build automata at run time (the .NET non-backtracking engine, RE\verb~|#~, derivre, the LLVM libc matcher) all do the opposite: every regex node is made by a constructor that applies the rewrite rules and looks the result up in a table of existing nodes, so equal regexes are the same node.}

\p{Chosen: constructors with a node table. A node is a small integer.}

\ul{
\li{Equality and hashing of a state are operations on integers, not on trees.}
\li{Each node stores whether it accepts the empty string, computed once.}
\li{The rules are those Paguroidea applies in its pass: union and intersection as sorted lists without duplicates (which makes them associative, commutative and idempotent), the unit and zero laws, \code{\verb~|(r*)* = r*~}, \code{\verb~|¬¬r = r~}, and merging of the character sets inside one union or intersection.}
}

\p{The tree the caller builds is separate from these nodes: it is an ordinary value with no table behind it, so building a regex needs no state. Compiling a list of rules turns the trees into nodes.}

\p{Not in the first version: the further rules of RE\verb~|#~ (factoring \code{\verb~|ab|ac~} into \code{\verb~|a(b|c)~}, absorption, merging of counted loops) and the subsumption tests of .NET. They reduce the number of states for some patterns and can be added to the constructors later without changing anything else.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-alphabet]{
\title{2. The input is bytes; the alphabet says what a unit is}

\p{The matcher always reads bytes. An alphabet tells, for a position, how many bytes the unit there takes and which class it is in. The regex tree, the node table and the matcher are generic in the alphabet; the trait is given in decision 5.}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|Bytes~}: a unit is one byte. A set is 256 bits.}
\li{\code{\verb~|Chars~}: a unit is one character, decoded from UTF-8 in the matching loop, one to four bytes. A set is held as the code points where membership changes; see below.}
}

\p{Both read the same kind of input, so both can take it in pieces; see "Reading input" and "Characters".}

\p{The classes are computed once for all rules of a matcher, by refining a partition with each set the rules mention (\code{\verb~|whole~} and \code{\verb~|refine~} of the trait), as .NET and RE\verb~|#~ do. Paguroidea computes classes per state, which suits generated code but not one table indexed by state and class.}

\p{\strong{How sets and classes of characters are held.} A set of characters gives every code point one of two values, in or out; the classes give every code point a class number. Both are a value for every code point that changes at only a few of them, so both are held as the code points where the value changes, in a map ordered by code point: \code{\verb~|WavlMap<u32, V>~} of \code{\verb~|std::collections::pure~}. The entry \code{\verb~|(k, v)~} says the value is \code{\verb~|v~} from \code{\verb~|k~} up to the next key. ICU's \code{\verb~|UnicodeSet~} holds a set the same way, in a sorted array.}

\pre{\verb~|
[a-zα-ω] as a set         0 → out,  61 → in,  7B → out,  3B1 → in,  3CA → out
the classes it leaves     0 → 0,    61 → 1,   7B → 0,    3B1 → 1,   3CA → 0, …
~}

\table{
\tr{\th{Operation}\th{How}\th{Cost}}
\tr{\td{is a character in a set}\td{the entry at or below its code point}\td{a logarithm of the number of changes}}
\tr{\td{give a range one value}\td{remove the keys inside it, insert its two ends, drop an end where the value does not change}\td{a logarithm, and the keys removed}}
\tr{\td{union, intersection, difference}\td{walk the set with fewer changes and give its ranges a value in the other}\td{the smaller set times a logarithm}}
\tr{\td{refine the classes with a set}\td{make the bounds of the set keys, then look only at the ranges inside the set}\td{follows the size of the set, not the number of classes}}
}

\p{A sorted list of ranges does each of these by walking both operands, which is as good for two sets of similar size. The map is chosen for what happens when the rules of a matcher are walked: many small sets, each refining classes that keep growing, where a list would walk every class for every set.}

\p{\strong{Why the WAVL map and not the Patricia map.} \code{\verb~|IntMap~} of the same library has every operation used here, and its shape depends on the keys alone. Both were measured under the same code for sets and classes, in one program, release profile, whole process, median of 11 runs in milliseconds:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Workload}\th{WAVL}\th{IntMap}\th{IntMap / WAVL}}
\tr{\td{refine the classes with 20,000 sets of one stretch}\td{81.1}\td{77.0}\td{0.95}}
\tr{\td{refine the classes with 200 sets of 200 stretches}\td{164.9}\td{168.4}\td{1.02}}
\tr{\td{one set as the union of 20,000 stretches}\td{24.8}\td{28.3}\td{1.14}}
\tr{\td{union of two sets of 1,000 stretches, 200 times}\td{96.4}\td{112.8}\td{1.17}}
\tr{\td{intersection of the same, 200 times}\td{90.7}\td{131.0}\td{1.45}}
\tr{\td{difference of the same, 200 times}\td{96.0}\td{124.5}\td{1.30}}
\tr{\td{complement, 200 times}\td{41.9}\td{54.4}\td{1.30}}
\tr{\td{5,000,000 lookups in a function of 2,000 stretches}\td{273.6}\td{300.1}\td{1.10}}
\tr{\td{a wide stretch united with a set of 2,000 stretches, 2,000 times}\td{144.3}\td{154.5}\td{1.07}}
\tr{\td{two unions and a comparison of sets of 2,000 stretches, 1,000 times}\td{114.9}\td{28.4}\td{0.25}}
}

\p{Refining the classes costs the same on both. Building sets, the set operations and lookups are 10 to 45 percent slower on \code{\verb~|IntMap~}: they are made of nearest-key lookups, which the balanced tree answers faster. \code{\verb~|IntMap~} wins twice. Comparing two maps is linear there, while \code{\verb~|WavlMap::equals~} looks every key up in the other map. And a fill over many keys can cut them out as one subtree with \code{\verb~|IntMap::range~}, which brings the wide-stretch row to 84.4 ms (0.59), while every row of small fills gets slower with that cut. Neither is on the path from rules to a matcher, so the WAVL map stays.}

\p{To know which classes a set cuts, the classes keep the number of characters in each class. A class is cut when fewer of its characters are inside the set than it has; its part inside takes the next free number, so the numbers stay without gaps and no class is renumbered. Surrogates and numbers above U+10FFFF are in no set and in no class.}

\p{The classes of \code{\verb~|Bytes~} are a list of at most 256 disjoint sets of 256 bits each.}

\p{\strong{Other representations of \code{\verb~|Bits~}, measured.} Two were tried against the four \code{\verb~|u64~} words: two \code{\verb~|u128~} words, and one \code{\verb~|Simd<[u64; 4]>~} with membership and the single-member set written directly on the vector. Instructions of each function compiled alone, for x86-64 without extensions and for \code{\verb~|x86-64-v3~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Function}\th{4 × u64}\th{2 × u128}\th{Simd}\th{4 × u64, v3}\th{2 × u128, v3}\th{Simd, v3}}
\tr{\td{union, intersection}\td{10}\td{10}\td{3}\td{10}\td{10}\td{2}}
\tr{\td{difference}\td{17}\td{17}\td{5}\td{13}\td{15}\td{2}}
\tr{\td{is empty}\td{5}\td{5}\td{7}\td{5}\td{5}\td{4}}
\tr{\td{number of members}\td{68}\td{68}\td{36}\td{9}\td{10}\td{17}}
\tr{\td{smallest member}\td{19}\td{17}\td{24}\td{16}\td{16}\td{24}}
\tr{\td{range}\td{67}\td{65}\td{148}\td{62}\td{61}\td{30}}
\tr{\td{one member}\td{66}\td{63}\td{18}\td{61}\td{59}\td{11}}
\tr{\td{membership}\td{72}\td{73}\td{13}\td{68}\td{69}\td{18}}
\tr{\td{equality}\td{11}\td{16}\td{7}\td{11}\td{15}\td{5}}
}

\p{Time against the four \code{\verb~|u64~} words, median of 7 runs:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Workload}\th{2 × u128}\th{Simd}\th{2 × u128, v3}\th{Simd, v3}}
\tr{\td{ranges and every operation mixed}\td{0.91}\td{0.71}\td{0.79}\td{0.26}}
\tr{\td{bitwise operations in a loop}\td{1.16}\td{1.02}\td{1.31}\td{1.16}}
\tr{\td{ranges}\td{0.87}\td{0.65}\td{0.81}\td{0.16}}
\tr{\td{membership of random numbers}\td{0.40}\td{0.12}\td{0.37}\td{0.10}}
\tr{\td{sets built one member at a time}\td{0.78}\td{0.17}\td{0.76}\td{0.14}}
\tr{\td{equality}\td{0.90}\td{0.59}\td{0.94}\td{0.58}}
\tr{\td{classes of bytes: refine with 24 ranges, then inside}\td{0.99}\td{1.06}\td{1.01}\td{1.05}}
}

\p{A \code{\verb~|u128~} is two 64-bit registers, so the bitwise functions are the same instructions; ranges clip two words where four were clipped and are somewhat faster, and equality is longer. The vector is two to ten times faster wherever a set is built or tested, and 5 to 6 percent slower in the classes of bytes, where the smallest member is asked for often. The vector is the better fit, and it is not used yet: membership and the smallest member take a lane by a computed index, which does not compile for a 32-bit target, see [prob-HP3C](prob-HP3C). The bitwise loop varies by a quarter between builds of the same code and decides nothing.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-actions]{
\title{3. A state is a vector of regexes; a match names rules, and actions are looked up by rule}

\p{As in Paguroidea, a state holds one node per rule, and a rule accepts in a state when its node accepts the empty string.}

\p{What the automaton produces is rule indices, nothing else:}

\pre{\verb~|
pub struct Match { start: u64, length: u64, rule: u32, accept: u32 }
~}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|rule~} is the winner: the earliest rule that accepts at the longest length.}
\li{\code{\verb~|accept~} identifies the set of all rules that accept there. \code{\verb~|lexer.rules(m)~} lists them in rule order. Two rules such as a keyword and an identifier both match \code{\verb~|while~}; the winner is the keyword, and the list has both.}
}

\p{Actions sit beside the automaton, in a table indexed by rule. The matcher is generic in their type \code{\verb~|T~} and offers three ways to use them, all built on \code{\verb~|Match~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Call}\th{Gives}\th{Use}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|lexer.next(source)~}}\td{the next \code{\verb~|Match~}}\td{the caller decides what to do with it}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|lexer.action(m)~}}\td{the value of type \code{\verb~|T~} stored for \code{\verb~|m.rule~}}\td{\code{\verb~|T~} is data, such as a token kind: the caller dispatches with \code{\verb~|match~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|lexer.fold(source, state)~}}\td{the final state}\td{\code{\verb~|T~} is a function from a state and a view of the matched text to a new state: the matcher calls the action of every match in turn}}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|fold~} is the dispatch form: each rule's function is called as its rule matches, and the state it threads can collect every match.}

\p{An action is given the matched text as a \code{\verb~|TextView~} of the standard library: a range of bytes together with the \code{\verb~|Text~} it lies in. The \code{\verb~|Text~} says who owns the bytes: \code{\verb~|Literal~} for a string literal, which nothing needs to keep alive, or \code{\verb~|Owned~} for a string, which the view shares. So a view stays valid for as long as the action or its state keeps it, whatever the matcher does next, and the action can tell which case it has from \code{\verb~|view.base()~}. The pointer and length the matcher itself reads through are never given to an action. A list of all matches is a fold that appends; \code{\verb~|lexer.matches(source)~} is that, for any \code{\verb~|T~}.}

\p{A rule may be marked as skipped, for white space and comments: \code{\verb~|next~} goes on to the following match, and \code{\verb~|fold~} does not call an action for it.}

\p{Why the automaton holds indices and not the values of \code{\verb~|T~}: states are compared and hashed while the table is built, which a \code{\verb~|T~} need not support; a set of accepting rules is a set of small integers; and a completed table does not depend on \code{\verb~|T~}.}

\p{A single regex is the case of one rule.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-table]{
\title{4. The table is built when rules become a matcher}

\p{Turning the rules into a matcher is the one place where everything is known, so the whole table is built there. Nothing is computed while matching, and a matcher is a value that never changes.}

\p{The table is compressed, and its compression is a property of all states together, which is why it cannot be filled one entry at a time:}

\pre{\verb~|
class_of[unit]                 -> column
row_of[state]                  -> row
next[row * columns + column]   -> state
accepting[state]               -> none, or the winning rule and the set of accepting rules
~}

\p{The steps, in order:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Classes.} Fold \code{\verb~|refine~} over every set in the rules (decision 2).}
\li{\strong{Nodes.} Turn the regex trees into nodes through the constructors (decision 1).}
\li{\strong{States.} Starting from the vector of the rules' nodes, take the derivative by every class and collect the vectors reached, until no new one appears. This stops with an error above a limit on the number of states, since some regexes have exponentially many.}
\li{\strong{Dead states.} A state is dead when no accepting state can be reached from it. With every transition known, this is one walk backwards from the accepting states. All dead states become the single dead state.}
\li{\strong{Columns.} Two classes whose columns are equal in every state are one column. The classes of step 1 come from the sets as written and may be finer than the automaton needs.}
\li{\strong{Rows.} Two states whose rows are equal share one row.}
\li{\strong{Runs.} For every state, the classes on which it loops to itself, and from them its run (decision 5).}
}

\p{Step 4 is what makes intersection and complement cost nothing extra. With them a state can match nothing without being the node \code{\verb~|∅~}: \code{\verb~|[a-z]+ & [0-9]+~} is such a regex. Asking that of a regex needs an emptiness test; asking it of the finished automaton is the walk of step 4, which is needed in any case to stop a match as early as possible.}

\p{A matcher is passed around freely and shared, because it is never updated:}

\pre{\verb~|
let (source, found) = lexer.next(source);
~}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-simd]{
\title{5. Decoding and acceleration belong to the alphabet}

\p{The matcher has no decoding and no vector code. Both are functions of the alphabet. The functions on classes are public; \code{\verb~|unit~}, \code{\verb~|run~} and \code{\verb~|skip~} are in a trait private to the module, shown here together:}

\pre{\verb~|
pub trait Alphabet {
    /// A set of units, as the builder takes it.
    type Set;
    /// A partition of the units into classes, and the map from a unit to its class.
    type Classes;
    /// A loop set in the form the skip of this alphabet uses.
    type Run;

    /// The partition with one class: nothing tells two units apart yet.
    fn whole() -> Self::Classes;
    /// Split every class into its part inside `set` and its part outside.
    fn refine(classes: Self::Classes, set: Self::Set) -> Self::Classes;
    /// The classes that make up `set`, which `classes` was refined with.
    fn inside(classes: Self::Classes, set: Self::Set) -> ClassSet;

    /// The unit that starts at `at`: its class and its width in bytes;
    /// or that the window ends inside it; or that no unit starts there.
    fn unit(classes: Self::Classes, window: Window, at: u64) -> Unit;

    /// How to skip a run of units of the classes in `looping`, or none when this
    /// alphabet has no skip for them.
    fn run(classes: Self::Classes, looping: ClassSet) -> Option<Self::Run>;
    /// The first position from `at` on where a unit outside the run starts, or the
    /// end of the last whole unit in the window.
    fn skip(run: Self::Run, window: Window, at: u64) -> u64;
}

pub enum Unit { Class(u32, u64), Incomplete, Malformed }
~}

\p{The trait takes and returns values only, and no collection appears in it. The classes of a matcher are a fold: start from \code{\verb~|whole()~} and \code{\verb~|refine~} with each set met while walking the regex trees of the rules. \code{\verb~|ClassSet~} is the matcher's own type, a set of class numbers; it is how a set node and a loop set are held once the classes are known. In the first version it is 256 bits, the record that also holds a set of bytes, so a matcher has at most 256 classes.}

\p{When a state is created, the matcher computes the classes on which the state loops to itself and asks the alphabet for a run. In the loop it calls \code{\verb~|skip~} for a state that has one. An alphabet that returns no run is matched one unit at a time, and nothing else changes. A skip must return a position where a unit starts.}

\p{\code{\verb~|Bytes~} gives a run in two forms. Vector code cannot be generated per regex the way Paguroidea generates it at compile time, so its shape is fixed and its constants are data.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Loop set or its complement}\th{How \code{\verb~|Bytes~} skips the run}}
\tr{\td{a few single bytes and ranges (the bound of Paguroidea: cost 4, one per byte, two per range)}\td{compare each chunk with \code{\verb~|splat~} of the stored bytes and bounds, combine the masks, and take \code{\verb~|first_set~} of the result}}
\tr{\td{any other set}\td{one byte at a time through a table of 256 bits stored with the run}}
}

\p{Both rows use operations that exist. A membership test for an arbitrary set of bytes on a whole vector at once would need a shuffle whose indices are a run-time vector, which \code{\verb~|core::simd~} does not have; it is not part of this design.}

\p{\code{\verb~|Chars~} may give no run, and is then matched one character at a time. It can also reuse the skip of \code{\verb~|Bytes~} on a set of bytes derived from the set of characters; see "Characters".}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-home]{
\title{6. Where it lives}

\p{A module \code{\verb~|std::regex~}, with nothing added to the compiler.}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-input]{
\title{Reading input}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-window]{
\title{The window}

\p{Inside one scan the matcher reads input through a window: a pointer to the first byte and a number of bytes.}

\pre{\verb~|
pub struct Window { base: ConstPtr<Generic>, len: u64 }      // len in bytes
~}

\p{A window holds a raw pointer, so it never leaves \code{\verb~|std::regex~}:}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|Window~} is private to the module. No public function takes or returns one.}
\li{The matcher makes a window from a \code{\verb~|TextView~} at the start of a scan and holds that view until the scan returns, so the pointer cannot outlive the bytes.}
\li{The functions that take a window, \code{\verb~|unit~} and \code{\verb~|skip~}, are in a trait that is private to the module, so they cannot be called or implemented outside it. \code{\verb~|#[sealed]~} does not serve here: it marks a trait whose impls the compiler provides.}
\li{What a caller sees is positions as numbers in a \code{\verb~|Match~} and text as a \code{\verb~|TextView~}.}
}

\p{The window is what makes looking ahead possible. A step reads the unit that starts at a position; a skip loads a vector of bytes starting at a position. Neither reads at or past \code{\verb~|len~}: the last chunk is loaded under the mask \code{\verb~|mask_first(len - at)~}, so no unit outside the window is touched and there is no scalar loop for the remainder.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-loop]{
\title{The matching loop}

\p{The position of a match in progress is three values:}

\pre{\verb~|
pub struct Scan { state: u32, at: u64, accepted: Option<Accept> }   // Accept: the rule and the length at which it accepted
~}

\p{One call advances a scan over the bytes a source holds:}

\pre{\verb~|
fn run(lexer, scan, held: TextView) -> (scan, Status)      // Status: Dead or Exhausted
window = the pointer and length of `held`

loop {
    if accepting[state] is a rule   { accepted = (rule, at) }
    if state has a run              { at = skip(run, window, at) ; if it moved and accepting[state] { accepted = (rule, at) } }
    if at == window.len             { return Exhausted }
    match unit(classes, window, at) {
        Incomplete                  => return Exhausted        // the window ends inside a character
        Malformed                   => return Dead
        Class(column, width)        => {
            to = next[row_of[state] * columns + column]
            if to is dead           { return Dead }
            state = to ; at = at + width
        }
    }
}
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|Dead~} means no rule can match further: the result is \code{\verb~|accepted~}. \code{\verb~|Exhausted~} means the window ended, possibly inside a character, while some rule could still match. For \code{\verb~|Bytes~}, \code{\verb~|unit~} is one load from the class map and its width is always one.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-skip]{
\title{The skip}

\p{For a state that loops on the bytes \code{\verb~|[a-z]~}, the skip of \code{\verb~|Bytes~} is this function. The bounds \code{\verb~|lo~} and \code{\verb~|hi~} are fields of the state's run.}

\pre{\verb~|
fn skip(lo: u8, hi: u8, base: ConstPtr<Generic>, at: u64, len: u64) -> u64 {
    let width = simd::lanes<Simd<[u8; [16]]>>();
    let (end, stop) = while [i = at, stop = false] (i < len && !stop) {
        let live   = simd::mask_first<Simd<[bool; [16]]>>(len - i);
        let chunk  = simd::load<Simd<[u8; [16]]>>(ptr::offset(base, i as i64, PtrArithFlag::Inbounds), live, simd::splat<Simd<[u8; [16]]>>(0));
        let inside = simd::ge(chunk, simd::splat<Simd<[u8; [16]]>>(lo)) & simd::le(chunk, simd::splat<Simd<[u8; [16]]>>(hi));
        let run    = simd::first_set(!(inside & live));      // units of the run at the front of this chunk
        (i + run, run < width)
    };
    end
}
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|first_set~} of the complement counts the leading bytes that are both loaded and in the set, so \code{\verb~|end~} is the first position whose byte is outside the set, or \code{\verb~|len~}. The vectors are local to one iteration; the loop carries only \code{\verb~|i~}.}

\p{This function is part of \code{\verb~|Bytes::skip~}. The matching loop above calls \code{\verb~|skip~} through the alphabet and never names a vector type.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-source]{
\title{Input that arrives in pieces}

\p{A source hands the matcher the bytes it holds, as a \code{\verb~|TextView~}, and can be asked for more. It is a public trait and has no pointer in it:}

\pre{\verb~|
pub trait Source {
    /// The bytes held from the start of the current token on, as a view that
    /// shares their owner.
    fn held(self: Self) -> TextView;
    /// Read more bytes after those held, keeping them. False at the end of input.
    fn extend(self: Self) -> (Self, bool);
    /// Drop the first `count` bytes: a token was returned.
    fn consume(self: Self, count: u64) -> Self;
}
~}

\p{A \code{\verb~|Text~}, literal or owned, is a source that holds all of it and never extends. The text of a match is the first \code{\verb~|length~} bytes of \code{\verb~|held()~}, a range of that \code{\verb~|Text~}. A buffered reader keeps the bytes of the current token in a string and appends to them; its views share that string. The source does not depend on the alphabet.}

\p{A view of an owned string shares it. If an action keeps such a view and the reader then needs to append, the string is no longer held by the reader alone, and the reader continues in a new string with the bytes not yet consumed. An action that only reads the view and lets it go costs nothing. This cost is accepted: it is what sharing means, and it is paid only by an action that keeps a view.}

\p{\code{\verb~|next~} is then:}

\pre{\verb~|
scan = start
loop {
    (scan, status) = run(lexer, scan, source.held())    // a window is made from the view inside `run`
    if status is Dead                    { break }
    (source, more) = source.extend()     // Exhausted: the window ended
    if !more                             { break }
}
return the action and length of scan.accepted, and source.consume(length)
~}

\p{The scan is kept across \code{\verb~|extend~}, so the matcher never reads a byte twice, and a skip that stopped at the end of one piece goes on in the next. The bytes of the token stay in the window until \code{\verb~|consume~}, because the longest match may end before the last byte read.}

\p{A character cut in two by the end of a piece needs no buffer of its own: \code{\verb~|unit~} reports \code{\verb~|Incomplete~}, the scan stays where the character starts, and after \code{\verb~|extend~} the window holds all of it.}

\p{For a caller that pushes pieces instead of being asked for them, the library provides a source that owns a buffer:}

\pre{\verb~|
let stream = Stream::new(lexer);
let (stream, matches) = stream.feed(piece);      // every match that is certain so far
let (stream, matches) = stream.finish();         // the end of input
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|Stream~} holds the scan and the bytes not yet consumed, beside the lexer. A match is certain once the scan is \code{\verb~|Dead~}; until then the bytes of the token in progress are kept.}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-characters]{
\title{Characters}

\p{Text is UTF-8: a character takes one to four bytes. A regex over characters can meet such text in three ways.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Way}\th{What the loop steps on}\th{States}\th{Per character}}
\tr{\td{decode in the loop}\td{characters}\td{one step per character}\td{a decode, and a search in the class map beyond ASCII}}
\tr{\td{translate the regex to bytes when it is built}\td{bytes}\td{more, for sets beyond ASCII}\td{one table load per byte}}
\tr{\td{decode the text first}\td{\code{\verb~|char~} units of four bytes}\td{one step per character}\td{the text is copied at four bytes per character}}
}

\p{Chosen: decode in the loop. Bytes and characters then share one kind of input and one matcher, and both take input in pieces.}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-decoding]{
\title{Decoding}

\p{\code{\verb~|Chars::unit~} at a position:}

\ol{
\li{The byte is below \code{\verb~|80~}: the class comes from a table of 128 entries, the width is one. Text that is mostly ASCII takes this path.}
\li{Otherwise the first byte gives the width. If the window ends before the last byte of the character, the answer is \code{\verb~|Incomplete~}.}
\li{The code point is assembled and its class found by binary search in the sorted bounds of the classes. The bounds are the changes of the classes, written out in order when the matcher is built.}
\li{A byte that cannot start a character, or a wrong continuation byte, is \code{\verb~|Malformed~}, which ends the match there.}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-char-skip]{
\title{Skipping, without decoding}

\p{A vector looks ahead on bytes, and a skip must stop where a character starts. That holds for a set of bytes derived from the loop set \code{\verb~|S~} of characters:}

\pre{\verb~|
bytes(S) = the ASCII characters of S, as bytes
           and all bytes 80 to FF, if S has every character beyond ASCII
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|Chars~} then skips with the skip of \code{\verb~|Bytes~} on \code{\verb~|bytes(S)~}.}

\p{Why the stop is where a character starts, in well-formed text and starting from such a position:}

\ul{
\li{If \code{\verb~|S~} has every character beyond ASCII, the skip stops only at an ASCII byte, and an ASCII byte is always a whole character.}
\li{Otherwise it stops at the first byte that is \code{\verb~|80~} or above, or at an ASCII byte outside \code{\verb~|S~}. Every byte before it in the run was ASCII, so that byte starts a character.}
}

\p{At the end of the window the skip may have run into a character that is cut in two; it then steps back to where that character starts, which is at most three bytes.}

\p{Two examples:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Loop set \code{\verb~|S~}}\th{\code{\verb~|bytes(S)~}}\th{Effect}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|[^"\\]~}, the inside of a string literal}\td{all bytes but the quote and the backslash}\td{the skip runs through text in any script and stops at a quote or a backslash}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|[a-zα-ω]~}}\td{\code{\verb~|[61-7A]~}}\td{the skip runs through ASCII letters; a Greek letter is decoded and takes one step, after which the skip resumes}}
}

\p{So \code{\verb~|Chars~} is accelerated exactly where its loop set is plain on the byte level, and falls back to one decoded step otherwise.}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-data]{
\title{Data}

\p{Nodes, in a table that makes equal nodes the same integer:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Node}\th{Children}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|∅~}}\td{none}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|ε~}}\td{none}}
\tr{\td{set}\td{a set of classes, as a bit per class}}
\tr{\td{concatenation}\td{two nodes}}
\tr{\td{star}\td{one node}}
\tr{\td{union, intersection}\td{a sorted list of nodes without duplicates}}
\tr{\td{complement}\td{one node}}
}

\p{A set node holds classes, not units: once the classes of a matcher are computed, every set is a union of classes, and the derivative of a set by a class is one bit test.}

\p{Tables of a matcher:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Table}\th{Indexed by}\th{Entry}}
\tr{\td{class map}\td{unit (256 entries for \code{\verb~|Bytes~}; for \code{\verb~|Chars~}, 128 entries for ASCII and a sorted list of bounds)}\td{class}}
\tr{\td{columns}\td{class}\td{column of the transition table}}
\tr{\td{rows}\td{state}\td{row of the transition table}}
\tr{\td{transitions}\td{row × column}\td{state, with one reserved value: dead}}
\tr{\td{accepting}\td{state}\td{none, or the winning rule and the set of accepting rules}}
\tr{\td{accepting sets}\td{set id}\td{the rules that accept, in rule order}}
\tr{\td{runs}\td{state}\td{none, or the run the alphabet gave for its loop set}}
\tr{\td{states}\td{state}\td{its vector of nodes; and the reverse map from a vector to its state}}
\tr{\td{rules}\td{rule index}\td{the action of type \code{\verb~|T~}, and whether the rule is skipped}}
}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-interface]{
\title{Interface sketch}

\p{Every value here is immutable; each call returns a new value. No collection is part of the interface: rules are added one at a time, and matches are consumed by a fold.}

\pre{\verb~|
import std::regex::{Regex, Bytes, Rules, Lexer};

let lower = Regex::<Bytes>::range(97, 122);
let digit = Regex::<Bytes>::range(48, 57);
let space = Regex::<Bytes>::byte(32);

// Data as actions: the caller dispatches.
let lexer = Rules::new()
    .rule(lower.plus(), Token::Ident)
    .rule(digit.plus(), Token::Number)
    .skip(space.plus())
    .lexer();

let (source, found) = lexer.next(source);       // Some(Match { start: 0, length: 3, rule: 0, .. })
match found {
    Some(m) => match lexer.action(m) { Token::Ident => ..., Token::Number => ... },
    None => ...
}

// Functions as actions: the matcher dispatches, over all matches.
let counter = Rules::new()
    .rule(lower.plus(), |words: u64, text: TextView| words + 1)
    .skip(space.plus())
    .lexer();
let words = counter.fold(source, 0);
~}

\table{
\tr{\th{Value}\th{Made by}\th{Used by}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Regex<A>~}}\td{\code{\verb~|empty~}, \code{\verb~|epsilon~}, \code{\verb~|set~}, \code{\verb~|byte~} or \code{\verb~|char~}, \code{\verb~|range~}, \code{\verb~|literal~}; and from regexes: \code{\verb~|then~}, \code{\verb~|or~}, \code{\verb~|and~}, \code{\verb~|not~}, \code{\verb~|star~}, \code{\verb~|plus~}, \code{\verb~|optional~}}\td{\code{\verb~|rule~}, \code{\verb~|skip~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Rules<A, T>~}}\td{\code{\verb~|Rules::new()~}, then \code{\verb~|rule(regex, action)~} and \code{\verb~|skip(regex)~}}\td{\code{\verb~|lexer()~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Lexer<A, T>~}}\td{\code{\verb~|rules.lexer()~}, which builds the table and fails above the state limit}\td{\code{\verb~|next~}, \code{\verb~|action~}, \code{\verb~|rules~}, \code{\verb~|fold~}, \code{\verb~|fold_matches~}, \code{\verb~|matches~}}}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|fold~} is the general way to consume matches: \code{\verb~|lexer.fold(source, state)~} with functions as actions, each from a state and a \code{\verb~|TextView~} to a state, or \code{\verb~|lexer.fold_matches(source, state, step)~} with a function \code{\verb~|step~} from a state, a \code{\verb~|Match~} and a \code{\verb~|TextView~} for any action type. \code{\verb~|matches~} is \code{\verb~|fold_matches~} that builds a list.}
}

\subtree[design-regex-derivatives-open]{
\title{Open questions}

\ul{
\li{Whether \code{\verb~|Chars~} skips from the first version on, or is matched one character at a time first.}
\li{What \code{\verb~|Malformed~} should do for input that is not known to be UTF-8: end the match, as here, or count the byte as a unit of its own class.}
\li{Whether "all matches" should also mean a search for one regex anywhere in the input. That needs an automaton for \code{\verb~|.*r~} and is left to a later version here.}
\li{Whether 256 classes are enough for \code{\verb~|Chars~}, or the set of classes has to grow with the rules.}
}
}
